<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wen Xuan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 530</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Selections of Refined Literature</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wen Xuan (Selections of Refined Literature) is an anthology of Chinese poetry and prose compiled in the early 6th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-tong">Xiao Tong</a>, crown prince of the Liang dynasty. Its 30 chapters, later divided into 60, gather some 700 works by about 130 writers from the Zhou to the Liang, arranged by genre. It became a standard text for scholars and examination candidates under the Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wen-xuan.jpg" alt="Wen Xuan" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: He Zhuo 何焯 (Life time: AD 1661 - 1722) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhao_Ming_Wen_Xuan,_He_edition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
